{
  "abstract": "We prove that a biplanar graph containing an eight-clique whose vertices have at most two outside neighbors admits a smaller biplanar replacement on the remaining vertices, every nine-coloring of which extends to the original graph. Hence a minimum-order biplanar graph requiring ten colors contains no eight-clique of degree-nine vertices. The proof combines Hall's theorem, established layerwise linking operations, and a constructive star-parity alternative that handles arbitrary disconnected outside-neighbor patterns. We also prove a regular-boundary coverage criterion for bounded extra attachments and a closed-boundary extension allowing one clique vertex to have total degree up to fifteen. The theorems place no common-face condition on the planar layers. Standard-library algorithms return independently checkable certificates, and four literal examples include original and replacement planar rotations. The general results have hand proofs and do not depend on SAT or a finite classification. The unrestricted Earth–Moon problem remains open.",
